er I read for a conservative  website and I went and it was quite an  interesting experience and it was just  lull in the day and I took that time  because usually when people are voting  is not much news so I took this couple  of hours and I talked about it a little  bit in the book but the tradition is to  write a note to leave at the grave site  of the Rebbe there are thousands and  thousands of notes at this grave site  the the jockey who won the Triple Crown  on American Pharoah left a note and he  went on to win the Triple Crown the next  day but anyway I left my note on the  sort of side of the grave area and I  intended to fold it up and put it on the  grave and then this his breeze came  through and sort of lifted it up and it  landed on the grave site which which to  me was kind of interesting anyway I got  an uber back into town to get ready for  the victory party and this uber got lost  in Queens and so I was stuck in Queens  for an hour which made me very nervous  about missing deadlines and so forth but  it was amazing to see the world Donald  Trump came from the and ring through  these streets and Queens that this is  who he is he's a guy who grew up in  Queens and made it in Manhattan and has  now achieved the ultimate ambition that  he or his family would have would have  imagined and so that was a very  interesting way to spend the afternoon  on Election Day of course when I got to  the Trump victory party that night it  felt like a funeral it was 7:30 at night  people looked a little bit dejected it  was a small issue at the Hilton or at  Trump Tower this was at the Hilton this  is that the media access to the victory  party and you know the donors were  gathering there and most of the people  you know the really inner circle was at  Trump Tower for a while but the Hilton  party felt very very diminished very sad  kind of quiet I talked to my Breitbart  colleagues there and they all had  explanations for why Trump was going to  lose people felt this wasn't looking  good the exit polls were terrible and so  forth and then we were looking at the  screen there was this map of Florida and  the Panhandle counties hadn't come  through yet now I just been there with  the Trump campaign in Pensacola and my  wife actually did her Navy training in  Pensacola so I know the area somewhat  well and that is deep deep red country  it's very very Republican in fact so  Republican that when I asked a woman at  this rally in Pensacola the same  question I asked people everywhere which  is why are you here what drew you to  Donald Trump I said to her why are you  here she said because I'm not an idiot I  said that was her that was her answer  that's how automatic the vote was in  those counties so I said well if they  haven't counted those counties yet how  do we know which way Florida is gonna go  and sure enough a short while later  those County started to come in the  state flipped into the Trump column and  suddenly everybody felt Trump was gonna  win  now I didn't think he was automatically  going to win because he was winning  Florida in fact I counted on him winning  Florida in my pre-election calculations  I knew that his strategy had to be to  pick off a blue state he was not going  to win by using the tried and tested and  often failed Republican strategy of just  winning the conservative states and  competing in the swing states there  about 12 swing states I knew that he was  going to have to steal at least one  state from the blue column and this has  been a Republican idea for quite a while  Sarah Palin wanted to do it in 2008 and  a campaign held her back from  campaigning in Michigan Mitt Romney and  Paul Ryan tried to swing Wisconsin this  has been an idea out there but Trump  really wanted to do it and the day  before the election I actually ran into  Frank Luntz I tell this story in the  book I ran into him on the outskirts of  a rally in North Carolina and I said to  him Frank can Trump win and he said yeah