 of  these other states oh yes oh yes yes  there were many people who started  streaming in at about 9:30 that night  and it was pandemonium for a long time  we were in the media gallery looking  down on the floor but it was kind of  like a mosh pit from about midnight to  3:00 in the morning before finally the  AP called the race and there was some  booing when John Podesta came out and  said they're going to still count some  more votes and postpone this to the next  day or whatever and people started  booing and then one network at coal  Pennsylvania so anyway lots of drama but  I think what's really interesting to  people is not so much the election night  dramatics which I think everyone has  their own interesting story to tell  about where were you when this happened  but some of the conversations I had with  people on the campaign trail with voters  with the people who were in those crowds  the people who  I liked Trump I first had a sense that  Trump could win the nomination here in  Las Vegas I went to an event this is  still in the primary in 2015 here in Las  Vegas and Trump did a couple of things  that no other candidate does the  conventional wisdom among politicians is  that you want to keep an audience  waiting you know that you're not a  celebrity in your own right and suppose  Trump is but very few politicians are  and so they worked hard to create an air  of mystique around themselves they don't  want to be immediately accessible to  everybody especially if they're trying  to raise money it want to be a little  bit distant so people want to be closer  to this person so I was told by some  campaign consultants that one of the  things more seasoned candidates do is  they arrive on time but they wait in  some backroom for 15 or 20 minutes and  then they come onstage Trump didn't do  that at the event here in Las Vegas he  just came right out on time and he did  that throughout the campaign he was  almost always on time and the message  that sends to people in the audience who  have driven who have waited camped out  by the door or whatever the message that  sends is that their time is worth  something to him so there was an  immediate intimacy between the candidate  in the audience which most politicians  don't want they want a kind of adulation  they want a little bit of a distance  they want the audience to put them on a  pedestal Donald Trump came off the  pedestal and then he would stand there  for an hour and speak extemporaneously  about whatever was on his mind now when  the media started paying attention to  what he was saying once he had clinched  the nomination I remember the first  campaign speech they covered they were  saying this man's crazy this is a stream  of consciousness Rambo this has no  coherence that's what all of his  speeches were like but it wasn't crazy  if you were standing in the room it was  a conversation that you would have among  friends about current events and it was  an ordinary kind of conversation and  people appreciated that he was letting  them into his confidence sharing his  inner reflections with them the way he  still does on Twitter and in the New  York Times even some of these interviews  he gives he lets on a little bit too  much about what he's really thinking  about things but I don't think we've had  a president who's been as direct and as  accessible in terms of  letting people know his own mind he  doesn't let you know what his policies  are gonna be especially in foreign  policy that's a it's a mantra almost in  the trumpet's world we're not going to  tell you what we're gonna do with Isis  or with Iran or with Russia and I think  it's good to be unpredictable that's my  personal view and it's good to be  unpredictable for strategic reasons but  in terms of how he feels about things  he's very direct about that and that  created a rapport between him and his  audience and as I left that event that  night in 2015 I said there's no other  Republican candidate that does that no  one who can do it  Trump drew that night about 4,000 or  5,000 people to the West Gate casino I  think it was Marco Rubio had a rally a  few blocks away and he had about 200 and  and Rubio on paper in a sense was a  better candidate he had more political  experience his policies lined up  perfectly with conservative movement  Republican Party he was very good on  television he was Latino he could appeal  to a new section of the electorate  Republicans have been struggling to  reach so in a sense on paper he was the  guy who checked all the boxes but Trump  had this connection to people and it  wasn't just celebrity it was really  something he knew how to do it wasn't  demagoguery either it wasn't kind of a  charismatic persona you know when you  looked at Obama rally 'he's there was  almost that kind of charismatic pall  that fell over the crowd almost a cult  of personality people believed in and  they elevated him above them Trump had a  different appeal he was the man who  would come down from the Trump Tower on  the escalator sort of descending and and  giving up a lot of that prestige look  how his brands have suffered look how  his personal life has been  opened up and turned upside down how his  business is now under investigation he's  exposed himself in all of these ways for  the sake of this job and there was a  young woman on the campaign trail at a  rally in Michigan actually was one of  the more interesting rallies of the  campaign it was just north of Detroit  and it was one of the few events that  started late started late because we had  trouble getting there through some of  the traffic on the roads around Detroit  and the Secret Service were literally  clearing people off the roads because I  hadn't done it yet and it was very long  route anyway got there late  it was absolutely packed it was one of  the younger audiences you know  conservative audiences tend to be a  little bit older this was a young  audience people in their late teens  early 20s was like a rock and roll  concert and I asked one of the young  women near the media area what brought  her here and why she liked Donald Trump  and she said he loves us and I said to  her how do you know he loves you and she  said because he doesn't have to do this  and that was the same sentiment I'd seen  at the beginning of the campaign when I  first went to a Trump event that people  had an appreciation for the way that he  was giving his time that he was arriving  on time that he was giving up his  lucrative comfortable life to enter the  fray of American politics now you might  say it's not such a great sacrifice he  lives at 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue it's  driven everywhere he's going to be  remembered in history his net worth is  probably risen as it does for all  presidents Obama is probably worth as  much as Mitt Romney at the moment which  is kind of ironic but the sense people  had was that he was sacrificing himself  for this it wasn't something that was  the next step up on a set of political  stairs the way it was for Hillary  Clinton and for other candidates  and I think that remains his connection  to his voting base that people feel that  he's giving something up for them and he  remains loyal to them and what's  remarkable in the first six months of  Trump's presidency is that despite the  daily ups and downs the roller coaster  ride that it's been through the media  and some of the setbacks in Congress and  so forth his own voters are almost  entirely 100 percent in lockstep still  with him and that's not because they  have a slavish cultish adherence to  Trump as a personality but it's because  he's delivering on things that he  promised them he would do it was very  interesting poll at the end of the first  hundred days that asked voters what they  thought about the Trump administration  and Democrats hated it Republicans loved  it but the one thing that both sides  agreed on was that he was doing what he  said he was going to do and Democrats  didn't like that he was doing what he  said he was going to do and Republicans  loved it but that was the one common  area of agreement and some of the polls  that came out around that time and I  think that's largely still true even  though he has met more obstacles  especially in Congress which I think was  unprepared for the trump presidency  hadn't really gotten all his ducks in a  row certainly I don't think that the  leaders of Congress expected Trump to  win I think some prepared for Trump to  win Mitch McConnell I think deserves a  lot of credit for the way he handled the  nomination of Merrick garland in 2016  and then Neal Gorsuch in 2017  he kind of held the line in the slim  chance that Trump was going to win and  he did so he made a big bet there but  mostly I think Washington was unprepared  so Trump is dealing with that he's  dealing with an extremely hostile media  he's dealing with a bureaucracy and an  administration that has portions of it  that are in not-so-secret rebellion  against his administration and I think  it's a massive challenge but so far he's  doing ok it's it's interesting to see  and certainly his voters feel that he's  delivering on what he promised them so  Pollak did you read Fear and Loathing in  Las Vegas before you know I didn't but  there's there's an element of Hunter s  Thompson in it although you know it's  not as crazy as that it's not it's not  as adventurous I talked a little bit in  the book about  some of the side trips I made while on  the campaign trail and one of the  interesting ones was to Harvard we  happen to be in New England and I flew  down from New Hampshire to Boston and I  joined some family friends there for the  Jewish Sabbath I observed the Jewish  Sabbath and I took that afternoon on  Saturday to walk across campus where I'd  studied and to visit some old friends  and one friend in particular who was a  quiet Trump supporter on campus there  were a few of them in the Harvard  faculty and we had this long long  discussion about Trump of what he meant  and especially about the perilous state  of American politics and American  political discourse and the thing that  worried us both at the time was that  whether Trump won or lost America was  going to be divided and it remains  divided remains bitterly divided and  it's not because of Trump it's not even  because of the campaign it goes back  earlier some of it starts in the Obama  administration some of it starts before  that but I remember almost with fondness  when I was in college in the 1990s the  big complaint among political  commentators were that the two parties  were too close to each other and that  American politics had become a kind of  consensus debate there was no real  disagreement there were no issues that  divided people that began to crack a  little bit with the impeachment and Bill  Clinton refusing to resign and then the  recount in 2000 so kind of looked back a  little a little ways to to see where  some of these divisions began but  certainly the Obama presidency was  divisive in that Obama  unlike Bill Clinton didn't react to  setbacks by coming toward the middle  whenever he had a political setback he  would move further in the direction he  had originally intended to go and I  think that for conservatives and  Republicans and many independents that  was frightening and so I think they  decided they needed someone to fight  back which was Trump's appeal in terms  of the style of his campaign  and here we are we have this agent of  change basically in the White House in a  way that Obama really wasn't Obama was  focused on changing the foundations of  society he spoke about the fundamental  transformation of the Constitution the  long arc of justice all this stuff  Obama was fixated on changing what he  saw as a society marred by the original  sin of slavery and racism and Trump  doesn't feel that way about America's  Founding he's trying to change America's  government and in a way that resonates  more deeply with the American electorate  which doesn't want to revisit its first  principles doesn't want to undo Liberty  doesn't want to apologize for the  American presence in the world but it  also causes a lot of problems in  Washington because government is set on  working a certain way and Trump wants it  to work differently Trump comes from the  real-estate world from a business world  where things are built where things are  done directly they're done early they're  done under budget Washington doesn't  work that way and there's this huge  clash of styles but the thing that  worried us in that little side trip I  took on the campaign trail remains  worrying to me now I wonder why we've  come as far down this road as we have  and one of the more interesting comments  in my book came from someone also a  Harvard professor who read it and and  told me that the thing he liked about my  book as someone from the outside was  that my personal account of being on the  campaign trail and following Trump made  Trump seemed normal to him because he is  not seen as normal in the media he's  either seen as you know terrible  dictatorial fascist whatever whatever  they apply to him or he's just seen as  this chimerical figure nobody can figure  out but when you are close to what's  going on and you can see the day-to-day  and you can place it in the broader  context of other people there at the  time and the geography and the changing  of the seasons whatever it is you  understand that there's an element of  this that is actually normal we've just  become used to a different way of doing  politics and maybe that's why people are  so frustrated with politics Trump is  trying to change that and some of those  changes are quite ambitious and maybe  challenging it sometimes  even radical but as a figure he's not  outside the bounds of the American  political experience and there's a lot  that's familiar about him if people  immerse themselves in 