islative side  then I think you'll see that trend  accelerated in how Trump won you talk  about as you said your experiences on  the campaign trail what was it  what's it like to be on the campaign  trail and what was the reaction you got  from the other reporters being a part of  bright bucks yeah well like Breitbart  fights with the media a lot so when I  joined the press playing I knew I wasn't  gonna make too many friends I was  surprised that I did make some friends  but I knew not to try to be too  colloquial with people because either  they wouldn't want that kind of  relationship with a Breitbart reporter  or maybe I wouldn't you know I had to  keep focused on on what I often felt  people weren't focusing on you know the  interesting thing to me was how few  journalists took an interest in the  crowds there was a kind of condescension  fear and irritation not every reporter  and not all the time but I found many  reporters stayed within the media pen  the sort of page they they put you in at  these big events and I would just jump  the fence I mean you know Secret Service  was there but you know I didn't really  care I was going to talk to people that  to me that was that's what made the news  story was talking to people at these  rallies and it struck me that there  wasn't that much curiosity about Trump  supporters that the media had an idea  already baked in about what Trump  supporters were I'm generalizing there  again not everybody was like that but  it was fun I mean there were some  friendships made across ideological  boundaries there were some very  left-wing reporters I got along very  well with because we talked about  baseball the Cubs were in the World  Series at the time I wore my Cubs hat  everywhere as a royal Cubs fan and it  was fun just to be on the trail many of  the reporters didn't like Trump but most  of them would agree and some some quite  open about it  that it was better to be covering Trump  than covering Hillary Clinton because 