er I read for a conservative  website and I went and it was quite an  interesting experience and it was just  lull in the day and I took that time  because usually when people are voting  is not much news so I took this couple  of hours and I talked about it a little  bit in the book but the tradition is to  write a note to leave at the grave site  of the Rebbe there are thousands and  thousands of notes at this grave site  the the jockey who won the Triple Crown  on American Pharoah left a note and he  went on to win the Triple Crown the next  day but anyway I left my note on the  sort of side of the grave area and I  intended to fold it up and put it on the  grave and then this his breeze came  through and sort of lifted it up and it  landed on the grave site which which to  me was kind of interesting anyway I got  an uber back into town to get ready for  the victory party and this uber got lost  in Queens and so I was stuck in Queens  for an hour which made me very nervous  about missing deadlines and so forth but  it was amazing to see the world Donald  Trump came from the and ring through  these streets and Queens that this is  who he is he's a guy who grew up in  Queens and made it in Manhattan and has  now achieved the ultimate ambition that  he or his family would have would have  imagined and so that was a very  interesting way to spend the afternoon  on Election Day of course when I got to  the Trump victory party that night it  felt like a funeral it was 7:30 at night  people looked a little bit dejected it  was a small issue at the Hilton or at  Trump Tower this was at the Hilton this  is that the media access to the victory  party and you know the donors were  gathering there and most of the people  you know the really inner circle was at  Trump Tower for a while but the Hilton  party felt very very diminished very sad  kind of quiet I talked to my Breitbart  colleagues there and they all had  explanations for why Trump was going to  lose people felt this wasn't looking  good the exit polls were terrible and so  forth and then we were looking at the  screen there was this map of Florida and  the Panhandle counties hadn't come  through yet now I just been there with  the Trump campaign in Pensacola and my  wife actually did her Navy training in  Pensacola so I know the area somewhat  well and that is deep deep red country  it's very very Republican in fact so  Republican that when I asked a woman at  this rally in Pensacola the same  question I asked people everywhere which  is why are you here what drew you to  Donald Trump I said to her why are you  here she said because I'm not an idiot I  said that was her that was her answer  that's how automatic the vote was in  those counties so I said well if they  haven't counted those counties yet how  do we know which way Florida is gonna go  and sure enough a short while later  those County started to come in the  state flipped into the Trump column and  suddenly everybody felt Trump was gonna  win  now I didn't think he was automatically  going to win because he was winning  Florida in fact I counted on him winning  Florida in my pre-election calculations  I knew that his strategy had to be to  pick off a blue state he was not going  to win by using the tried and tested and  often failed Republican strategy of just  winning the conservative states and  competing in the swing states there  about 12 swing states I knew that he was  going to have to steal at least one  state from the blue column and this has  been a Republican idea for quite a while  Sarah Palin wanted to do it in 2008 and  a campaign held her back from  campaigning in Michigan Mitt Romney and  Paul Ryan tried to swing Wisconsin this  has been an idea out there but Trump  really wanted to do it and the day  before the election I actually ran into  Frank Luntz I tell this story in the  book I ran into him on the outskirts of  a rally in North Carolina and I said to  him Frank can Trump win and he said yeah  he can win if he wins Nevada New  Hampshire Ohio in Florida Ohio I felt he  was gonna win Florida I thought his  chances were 50/50 New Hampshire I  thought also 5050 but Nevada I thought  Trump was going to lose because the  early vote for Democrats had already  come in and it was massive and I said  him well can he lose Nevada and win one  of the blue states and he looked at me  like I had fallen off the moon and he  said which one and he just couldn't  imagine of course the next day he  1/3 of those blue states he wanted  Pennsylvania Michigan and Wisconsin  Wisconsin was really the one that  clinched it and it was the one that know  pollsters saw Larry Schweikart my  co-author who was running his own  election models said that his team  predicted Trump's victory everywhere  except Wisconsin so everywhere it  happened they were right but they did  not think Wisconsin was gonna come into  the red column and it did and once  Wisconsin lit up then I said okay now  you can pop the champagne corks this is  over but of course it took three or four  more hours for the network's to call it  and standing there in the media gallery  you know around me was the press corps  and people look to kind of crestfallen  but they they did their jobs and and we  were sort of left at the end the  Breitbart crew watching all of this and  realizing suddenly that Donald Trump  would be the next president of United  States and we've covered this and in a  sense covered the conservative arguments  as well as some of the liberal ones  against Trump but we'd been in the  middle of a fight because Breitbart had  been one of the more supportive  conservative outlets of Trump's  candidacy and suddenly the reality of  what had happened hit us and it was just  this remarkable moment in history so it  was a real privilege to be there and I  write about that and I write about a lot  of that drama in the last few weeks in  the book did the party start to fill up  once Florida came in and once some of  these other states oh yes oh yes yes  there were many people who started  streaming in at about 9:30 that night  and it was pandemonium for a long time  we were in the media gallery looking  down on the floor but it was kind of  like a mosh pit from about midnight to  3:00 in the morning before finally the  AP called the race and there was some  booing when John Podesta came out and  said they're going to still count some  more votes and postpone this to the next  day or whatever and people started  booing and then one network at coal  Pennsylvania so anyway lots of drama but  I think what's really interesting to  people is not so much the election night  dramatics which I think everyone has  their own interesting story to tell  about where were you when this happened  but some of the conversations I had with  people on the campaign trail with voters  with the people who were in those crowds  the people who  I liked Trump I first had a sense that  Trump could win the nomination here in  Las Vegas I went to an event this is  still in the primary in 2015 here in Las  Vegas and Trump did a couple of things  that no other candidate does the  conventional wisdom among politicians is  that you want to keep an audience  waiting you know that you're not a  celebrity in your own right and suppose  Trump is but very few politicians are  and so they worked hard to create an air  of mystique around themselves they don't  want to be immediately accessible to  everybody especially if they're trying  to raise money it want to be a little  bit distant so people want to be closer  to this person so I was told by some  campaign consultants that one of the  things more seasoned candidates do is  they arrive on time but they wait in  some backroom for 15 or 20 minutes and  then they come onstage Trump didn't do  that at the event here in Las Vegas he  just came right out on time and he did  that throughout the campaign he was  almost always on time and the message  that sends to people in the audience who  have driven who have waited camped out  by the door or whatever the message that  sends is that their time is worth  something to him so there was an  immediate intimacy between the candidate  in the audience which most politicians  don't want they want a kind of adulation  they want a little bit of a distance  they want the audience to put them on a  pedestal Donald Trump came off the  pedestal and then he would stand there  for an hour and speak extemporaneously  about whatever was on his mind now when  the media started paying attention to  what he was saying once he had clinched  the nomination I remember the first  campaign speech they covered they were  saying this man's crazy this is a stream  of consciousness Rambo this has no  coherence that's what all of his  speeches were like but it wasn't crazy  if you were standing in the room it was  a conversation that you would have among  friends about current events and it was  an ordinary kind of conversation and  people appreciated that he was letting  them into his confidence sharing his  inner reflections with them the way he  still does on Twitter and in the New  York Times even some of these interviews  he gives he lets on a little bit too  much about what he's really thinking  about things but I don't think we've had  a president who's been as direct and as  accessible in terms of  letting people know his own mind he  doesn't let you know what his policies  are gonna be especially in foreign  policy that's a it's a mantra almost in  the trumpet's world we're not going to  tell you what we're gonna do with Isis  or with Iran or with Russia and I think  it's good to be unpredictable that's my  personal view and it's good to be  unpredictable for strategic reasons but  in terms of how he feels about things  he's very direct about that and that  created a rapport between him and his  audience and as I left that event that  night in 2015 I said there's no other  Republican candidate that does that no  one who can do it  Trump drew that night about 4,000 or  5,000 people to the West Gate casino I  think it was Marco Rubio had a rally a  few blocks away and he had about 200 and  and Rubio on paper in a sense was a  better candidate he had more political  experience his policies lined up  perfectly with conservative movement  Republican Party he was very good on  television he was Latino he could appeal  to a new section of the electorate  Republicans have been struggling to  reach so in a sense on paper he was the  guy who checked all the boxes but Trump  had this connection to people and it  wasn't just celebrity it was really  something he knew how to do it wasn't  demagoguery either it wasn't kind of a  charismatic persona you know when you  looked at Obama rally 'he's there was  almost that kind of charismatic pall  that fell over the crowd almost a cult  of personality people believed in and  they elevated him above them Trump had a  different appeal he was the man who  would come down from the Trump Tower on  the escalator sort of descending and and  giving up a lot of that prestige look  how his brands have suffered look how  his personal life has been  opened up and turned upside down how his  business is now under investigation he's  exposed himself in all of these ways for  the sake of this job and there was a  young woman on the campaign trail at a  rally in Michigan actually was one of  the more interesting rallies of the  campaign it was just north of Detroit  and it was one of the few events that  started late started late because we had  trouble getting there through some of  the traffic on the roads around Detroit  and the Secret Service were literally  clearing people off the roads because I  hadn't done it yet and it was very long  route anyway got there late  it was absolutely packed it was one of  the younger audiences you know  conservative audiences tend to be a  little bit older this was a young  audience people in their late teens  early 20s was like a rock and roll  concert and I asked one of the young  women near the media area what brought  her here and why she liked Donald Trump  and she said he loves us and I said to  her how do you know he loves you and she  said because he doesn't have to do this  and that was the same sentiment I'd seen  at the beginning of the campaign when I  first went to a Trump event that people  had an appreciation for the way that he  was giving his time that he was arriving  on time that he was giving up his  lucrative comfortable life to enter the  fray of American politics now you might  say it's not such a great sacrifice he  lives at 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue it's  driven everywhere he's going to be  remembered in history his net worth is  probably risen as it does for all  presidents Obama is probably worth as  much as Mitt Romney at the moment which  is kind of ironic but the sense people  had was that he was sacrificing himself  for this it wasn't something that was  the next step up on a set of political  stairs the way it was for Hillary  Clinton and for other candidates  and I think that remains his connection  to his voting base that people feel that  he's giving something up for them and he  remains loyal to them and what's  remarkable in the first six months of  Trump's presidency is that despite the  daily ups and downs the roller coaster  ride that it's been through the media  and some of the setbacks in Congress and  so forth his own voters are almost  entirely 100 percent in lockstep still  with him and that's not because they  have a slavish cultish adherence to  Trump as a personality but it's because  he's delivering on things that he  promised them he would do it was very  interesting poll at the end of the first  hundred days that asked voters what they  thought about the Trump administration  and Democrats hated it Republicans loved  it but the one thing that both sides  agreed on was that he was doing what he  said he was going to do and Democrats  didn't like that he was doing what he  said he was going to do and Republicans  loved it but that was the one common  area of agreement and some of the polls  that came out around that time and I  think that's largely still true even  though he has met more obstacles  especially in Congress which I think was  unprepared for the trump presidency  hadn't really gotten all his ducks in a  row certainly I don't think that the  leaders of Congress expected Trump to  win I think some prepared for Trump to  win Mitch McConnell I think deserves a  lot of credit for the way he handled the  nomination of Merrick garland in 2016  and then Neal Gorsuch in 2017  he kind of held the line in the slim  chance that Trump was going to win and  he did so he made a big bet there but  mostly I think Washington was unprepared  so Trump is dealing with that he's  dealing with an extremely hostile media  he's dealing with a bureaucracy and an  administration that has portions of it  that are in not-so-secret rebellion  against his administration and I think  it's a massive challenge but so far he's  doing ok it's it's interesting to see  and certainly his voters feel that he's  delivering on what he promised them so  Pollak did you read Fear and Loathing in  Las Vegas before you know I didn't but  there's there's an element of Hunter s  Thompson in it although you know it's  not as crazy as that it's not it's not  as adventurous I talked a little bit in  the book about  some of the side trips I made while on  the campaign trail and one of the  interesting ones was to Harvard we  happen to be in New England and I flew  down from New Hampshire to Boston and I  joined some family friends there for the  Jewish Sabbath I observed the Jewish  Sabbath and I took that afternoon on  Saturday to walk across campus where I'd  studied and to visit some old friends  and one friend in particular who was a  quiet Trump supporter on campus there  were a few of them in the Harvard  faculty and we had this long long  discussion about Trump of what he meant  and especially about the perilous state  of American politics and American  political discourse and the thing that  worried us both at the time was that  whether Trump won or lost America was  going to be divided and it remains  divided remains bitterly divided and  it's not because of Trump it's not even  because of the campaign it goes back  earlier some of it starts in the Obama  administration some of it starts before  that but I remember almost with fondness  when I was in college in the 1990s the  big complaint among political  commentators were that the two parties  were too close to each other and that  American politics had become a kind of  consensus debate there was no real  disagreement there were no issues that  divided people that began to crack a  little bit with the impeachment and Bill  Clinton refusing to resign and then the  recount in 2000 so kind of looked back a  little a little ways to to see where  some of these divisions began but  certainly the Obama presidency was  divisive in that Obama  unlike Bill Clinton didn't react to  setbacks by coming toward the middle  whenever he had a political setback he  would move further in the direction he  had originally intended to go and I  think that for conservatives and  Republicans and many independents that  was frightening and so I think they  decided they needed someone to fight  back which was Trump's appeal in terms  of the style of his campaign  and here we are we have this agent of  change basically in the White House in a  way that Obama really wasn't Obama was  focused on changing the foundations of  society he spoke about the fundamental  transformation of the Constitution the  long arc of justice all this stuff  Obama was fixated on changing what he  saw as a society marred by the original  sin of slavery and racism and Trump  doesn't feel that way about America's  Founding he's trying to change America's  government and in a way that resonates  more deeply with the American electorate  which doesn't want to revisit its first  principles doesn't want to undo Liberty  doesn't want to apologize for the  American presence in the world but it  also causes a lot of problems in  Washington because government is set on  working a certain way and Trump wants it  to work differently Trump comes from the  real-estate world from a business world  where things are built where things are  done directly they're done early they're  done under budget Washington doesn't  work that way and there's this huge  clash of styles but the thing that  worried us in that little side trip I  took on the campaign trail remains  worrying to me now I wonder why we've  come as far down this road as we have  and one of the more interesting comments  in my book came from someone also a  Harvard professor who read it and and  told me that the thing he liked about my  book as someone from the outside was  that my personal account of being on the  campaign trail and following Trump made  Trump seemed normal to him because he is  not seen as normal in the media he's  either seen as you know terrible  dictatorial fascist whatever whatever  they apply to him or he's just seen as  this chimerical figure nobody can figure  out but when you are close to what's  going on and you can see the day-to-day  and you can place it in the broader  context of other people there at the  time and the geography and the changing  of the seasons whatever it is you  understand that there's an element of  this that is actually normal we've just  become used to a different way of doing  politics and maybe that's why people are  so frustrated with politics Trump is  trying to change that and some of those  changes are quite ambitious and maybe  challenging it sometimes  even radical but as a figure he's not  outside the bounds of the American  political experience and there's a lot  that's familiar about him if people  immerse themselves in this Trump world  with maybe some people don't want to do  but if if you don't want to go to a  trump rally and didn't want to do that  and get yourself inside the Trump's mind  to think about policy the way he does  you might want to read the book because  that's the part of the book I really  focused on was describing the world that  traveled with Trump and Larry Schweikart  his part of the book is really focused  on the historical context of Trump so he  really puts it in context how the Trump  campaign although different from  anything anyone has experienced  certainly in our lifetimes is not  totally unprecedented there are  presidents with this kind of campaign  for some of these kinds of ideas and  after giving the history because Larry's  a historian he also gives you the data  that further explained why Trump won and  how Trump won and by the way he's still  collecting some of that same data he  says that the shift from Democrat to  Republican in voter registration has  actually continued since the 2016  election that the momentum is still on  the Republican side even though if you  watch the news you might think it was  the opposite but the electorate is  following Trump on the issues and  Democrats aren't talking about issues  right now they're talking about Trump  and that's good at solidifying their  base it's good at bringing in donations  it's not good at broadening the  Democratic Party electorate and that's  why Democrats are still struggling to  figure out their strategies in the  midterms of 2018 that's why they're  almost paralyzed by this resistance  movement which they encourage but which  is so radical that Democratic Party  leaders are having trouble reigning it  in Trump's talking about the issues  people still care about trade national  security health care and  that's why the electric continues to  trend in this direction we'll see how  much longer that lasts but if you can  start delivering on more of these things  particularly on the legislative side  then I think you'll see that trend  accelerated in how Trump won you talk  about as you said your experiences on  the campaign trail what was it  what's it like to be on the campaign  trail and what was the reaction you got  from the other reporters being a part of  bright bucks yeah well like Breitbart  fights with the media a lot so when I  joined the press playing I knew I wasn't  gonna make too many friends I was  surprised that I did make some friends  but I knew not to try to be too  colloquial with people because either  they wouldn't want that kind of  relationship with a Breitbart reporter  or maybe I wouldn't you know I had to  keep focused on on what I often felt  people weren't focusing on you know the  interesting thing to me was how few  journalists took an interest in the  crowds there was a kind of condescension  fear and irritation not every reporter  and not all the time but I found many  reporters stayed within the media pen  the sort of page they they put you in at  these big events and I would just jump  the fence I mean you know Secret Service  was there but you know I didn't really  care I was going to talk to people that  to me that was that's what made the news  story was talking to people at these  rallies and it struck me that there  wasn't that much curiosity about Trump  supporters that the media had an idea  already baked in about what Trump  supporters were I'm generalizing there  again not everybody was like that but  it was fun I mean there were some  friendships made across ideological  boundaries there were some very  left-wing reporters I got along very  well with because we talked about  baseball the Cubs were in the World  Series at the time I wore my Cubs hat  everywhere as a royal Cubs fan and it  was fun just to be on the trail many of  the reporters didn't like Trump but most  of them would agree and some some quite  open about it  that it was better to be covering Trump  than covering Hillary Clinton because  Hillary Clinton did very few events and  there wasn't much access to the  candidate and there was not much change  from day to day the speeches were  scripted that the crowds were bused in  and it was all the setup set pieces of  politics that you're used to  Trump was different you never knew what  was going to happen you never knew which  protester was going to show up there  were four or five events a day he was  going all over the place  it was absolutely exhausting
 

